WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.235
Lesson 3.4.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.350
Borrowing strategies & looping.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:06.759
By the end of this lesson, you'll be able

00:00:06.759 --> 00:00:10.085
to use borrowing deliberately, and know when looping adds

00:00:10.085 --> 00:00:12.035
return and when it just adds risk.

00:00:13.335 --> 00:00:14.625
Here's the plan.

00:00:14.625 --> 00:00:16.310
First, the key ideas.

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:19.025
Then, a worked example, step by step.

00:00:19.025 --> 00:00:20.550
Then, your checklist.

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:22.712
And finally, a three-question quiz.

00:00:24.012 --> 00:00:28.363
First, Liquidity without selling: borrow stablecoins against

00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:31.003
assets you want to keep (Module 12.3

00:00:31.003 --> 00:00:33.657
turns this into a credit policy).

00:00:33.657 --> 00:00:36.223
Next, Looping: deposit → borrow →

00:00:36.223 --> 00:00:39.692
re-deposit → repeat, to multiply exposure.

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:44.488
After n loops at LTV L: leverage =

00:00:44.488 --> 00:00:48.077
(1 − L^(n+1)) ÷ (1 − L).

00:00:48.077 --> 00:00:52.540
Then, Net APY on equity = collateral yield ×

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.807
leverage − borrow rate × (leverage − 1).

00:00:56.807 --> 00:01:00.243
And finally, Correlated loops (an LST against

00:01:00.243 --> 00:01:04.277
its underlying, or stablecoin against stablecoin) are

00:01:04.277 --> 00:01:07.639
far safer than volatile-against-stable loops.

00:01:08.940 --> 00:01:10.910
Let's look at the chart: Leveraged

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:12.955
loop: the spread is everything.

00:01:12.955 --> 00:01:14.515
Take a moment with it.

00:01:14.515 --> 00:01:16.925
It's everything we've just said, in one picture.

00:01:18.225 --> 00:01:20.740
Let's work an example, step by step.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:28.083
LTV 0.7, 3 loops, 3.5% collateral yield, 2.5% borrow:

00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:35.590
the calculator → 2.53× leverage, 5.03% net on equity.

00:01:35.590 --> 00:01:37.718
The return is wiped out

00:01:37.718 --> 00:01:40.235
if borrow rises to 5.78%.

00:01:40.235 --> 00:01:44.080
Section 3.1 showed borrow rates can jump

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:46.631
from 4% to 34% in hours.

00:01:46.631 --> 00:01:47.526
That's the risk.

00:01:48.826 --> 00:01:50.286
Here's your checklist.

00:01:50.286 --> 00:01:51.821
Do it now, for real.

00:01:51.821 --> 00:01:54.746
First, Repayment source named before borrowing.

00:01:54.746 --> 00:01:58.988
Next, Loops only on correlated pairs, below max leverage.

00:01:58.988 --> 00:02:02.363
And finally, Break-even borrow rate known and alerted.

00:02:03.663 --> 00:02:04.873
Question 1.

00:02:04.873 --> 00:02:08.573
Max leverage at LTV 0.75?

00:02:12.993 --> 00:02:14.608
The answer: 4×.

00:02:15.908 --> 00:02:17.153
Question 2.

00:02:17.153 --> 00:02:19.042
What single number decides whether

00:02:19.042 --> 00:02:20.068
a loop is worth it?

00:02:24.488 --> 00:02:26.229
The answer: The spread between

00:02:26.229 --> 00:02:28.083
collateral yield and borrow rate.

00:02:29.383 --> 00:02:30.653
Question 3.

00:02:30.653 --> 00:02:32.428
Why prefer correlated loops?

00:02:36.848 --> 00:02:40.013
The answer: Collateral and debt move together,

00:02:40.013 --> 00:02:42.168
so price moves barely change HF.

00:02:43.468 --> 00:02:44.713
Let's recap.

00:02:44.713 --> 00:02:49.111
Liquidity without selling: borrow stablecoins against assets

00:02:49.111 --> 00:02:51.706
you want to keep (Module 12.3 turns

00:02:51.706 --> 00:02:53.953
this into a credit policy).

00:02:53.953 --> 00:02:56.710
Looping: deposit → borrow → re-deposit

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:59.202
→ repeat, to multiply exposure.

00:02:59.202 --> 00:03:03.139
Net APY on equity = collateral yield ×

00:03:03.139 --> 00:03:07.477
leverage − borrow rate × (leverage − 1).

00:03:07.477 --> 00:03:11.387
Correlated loops (an LST against its underlying.

00:03:12.687 --> 00:03:15.202
Do the checklist now, before you move on.

00:03:15.202 --> 00:03:19.962
Next up, Lesson 3.5: Perpetual futures and margin on-chain.
